<section class="section" id="section-faq">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section__header">
      <span class="section__label">FAQ</span>
      <div class="accent-line"></div>
      <h2 class="section__title">Organic Pet Clothing Manufacturer | Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div style="max-width: 800px;">
      
      <div class="faq-item" role="region">
        <button class="faq-item__question" aria-expanded="false">
          What is the difference between GOTS and OCS certification?
          <span class="faq-item__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-item__answer" hidden>
          <p>GOTS requires at least 70% certified organic fibre and covers the entire processing chain including social and environmental criteria, chemical restrictions, and a maximum 10% synthetic blend. OCS only verifies organic fibre content (5-100%) with no processing, social, or chemical requirements. A product can carry an OCS label without meeting GOTS's stricter manufacturing standards, which is why GOTS is considered the stronger certification for organic claims.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="faq-item" role="region">
        <button class="faq-item__question" aria-expanded="false">
          How can I verify a GOTS certification is real?
          <span class="faq-item__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-item__answer" hidden>
          <p>Ask for the current-year Scope Certificate, which lists the facility, certification body, and valid date range, and a shipment-specific Transaction Certificate that ties the certified material to your actual order. GOTS certificates expire annually, so a 2024 certificate shown in 2026 is not valid. Certificates from Control Union, the certifying body operating in Bangladesh and India, carry a QR code linking to the original document. You can also search the company name directly in the GOTS public database at global-standard.org.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="faq-item" role="region">
        <button class="faq-item__question" aria-expanded="false">
          What is the minimum order quantity for organic pet clothing?
          <span class="faq-item__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-item__answer" hidden>
          <p>300 pieces per style, which is in line with the industry-standard range of 300-500 units for certified organic cotton production. Organic cotton typically carries higher MOQs than conventional cotton because certified fibre requires separate, audited processing batches to maintain chain-of-custody. This applies per style | colourways of the identical pattern and fabric are usually treated as one style.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="faq-item" role="region">
        <button class="faq-item__question" aria-expanded="false">
          Why does organic pet clothing cost more than conventional?
          <span class="faq-item__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-item__answer" hidden>
          <p>Certified organic cotton costs more at the farm level due to lower-yield organic farming methods, and the supply chain requires separate audited handling at every stage (ginning, spinning, dyeing) to prevent mixing with conventional fibre. Annual certification audits and Transaction Certificate documentation add further cost. Expect organic cotton garments to run 20-60% higher per unit than the same design in standard cotton-polyester blends.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="faq-item" role="region">
        <button class="faq-item__question" aria-expanded="false">
          Can organic pet clothing also be GRS certified for recycled content?
          <span class="faq-item__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-item__answer" hidden>
          <p>Yes, but they certify different things. GOTS covers organic fibre content and processing. GRS covers recycled content (minimum 20%) and processing. A garment can combine GOTS-certified organic cotton with GRS-certified recycled polyester trim or fill, but each material component needs its own valid certification rather than one certificate covering the whole product.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

